   PPrreevviioouussllyy ddeepprreeccaatteedd ssyyssrreeaadd(())//ssyysswwrriittee(()) oonn ::uuttff88 hhaannddlleess iiss nnooww ffaattaall
       Calling ssyyssrreeaadd(()), ssyysswwrriittee(()), sseenndd(()) or rreeccvv(()) on a ":utf8" handle,
       whether applied explicitly or implicitly, is now fatal.  This was
       deprecated in perl 5.24.

       There were two problems with calling these functions on ":utf8"
       handles:

       •   All four functions only paid attention to the ":utf8" flag.  Other
           layers were completely ignored, so a handle with
           :encoding(UTF-16LE) layer would be treated as UTF-8.  Other layers,
           such as compression are completely ignored with or without the
           ":utf8" flag.

       •   ssyyssrreeaadd(()) and rreeccvv(()) would read from the handle, skipping any
           validation by the layers, and do no validation of their own.  This
           could lead to invalidly encoded perl scalars.

       [GH #14839] <https://github.com/Perl/perl5/issues/14839>.

